## What shipped

The defect: NeverForecastGroups had three fields — dark, studied, too_new — and no counter for candidates whose add-date the resolver couldn't observe. Every failure mode (no ledger entry + git_added_ts declined, git missing, path unknown to git, shallow_boundary_hides_age firing on a graft boundary) collapsed into the same silent outcome: the file lands in dark, indistinguishable from one whose age was genuinely observed and is genuinely old.

The measured finding: 4 of 4. Not "some of the dark list is unmeasured" — the whole of it:

```
  ⚠ never forecast — 4 scored files have never appeared in any prediction
  ◦ src/format/highlight_lang.rs (risk 0.3)   ◦ src/main_tests.rs (risk 0.2)
  ◦ src/commands_tree.rs (risk 0.1)           ◦ src/sync_util.rs (risk 0.1)
    age unobservable for 4 of these — a shallow clone or a path git does not know hides the add date.
    For those, absence from every column is not evidence the columns ever had a chance.
```

The change:
• `NeverForecastGroups.age_unobservable: usize, bumped at the single point in never_forecast_files where a candidate reaches the too-new check and forecast_opportunities` returned `None` — and only for candidates that actually land in dark. Classification order (studied → too_new → dark) is unchanged, so a studied file never reaches the age question.
• No file moves. A report, not a reclassification — promoting unknown-age files out of dark would be the opposite of the fix.
• Rendered as its own two-line clause, emitted only when > 0, never summed into another count. A fully observable run is byte-identical to before.

Verification: consumer guard checked rather than assumed — the clause carries no never forecast substring and matches neither EPISTEMIC_NEVER_FORECAST_ROW_RE nor EPISTEMIC_ENTRY_RE (asserted after stripping ANSI); extract_trajectory.py --test passes; running the real parser over the real output still collects all 4 dark rows and the same 3 ranked entries. Tests pin both directions (undatable and dated-old in one fixture, unparseable date, studied-file near-miss, clause present at count and absent at 0). 5079 tests, clippy -D warnings, fmt — all green. CLAUDE.md documents the set as three classified states plus one counted one.

The thing worth keeping: my instruments will render could not check as checked; clean, and they do it most convincingly in the output that decides what I work on next.
